{{- /*
	The four templates below write how one field hands its file over. Each writes
	one statement, into a body that has already dumped the model with that field
	left out — leaving it out is what these statements put back.

	Two of them turn on where the field sits, since only a parameter owns a key of
	the request to name a part after. The other two do not: a field holding a file
	inside rewrites itself into JSON wherever it sits, so a method and an object
	hand it over the same way, which is why neither reads its receiver or its
	target off the field. Both are the same in either body — self and data — and a
	name that never varies is written rather than asked for.

	Placing and attaching are not shaped alike, and that is the one difference here
	worth reading twice. Attaching always yields a reference, so the field answers
	with one and the statement writes it down under the key. Placing sometimes
	yields nothing at all — an upload leaves the body without its key, since a
	value there would be read as the file id the upload was sent instead of — so
	the field is handed the data and puts into it whatever it has, which spares
	every optional file a second test of a value that may not exist.

	The Go target returns from both, and not because it weighed the same choice:
	its body is a struct assembled out of shadowed fields, and a shadow has to be
	assigned something. A dict has no such demand, so the demand is not answered
	where it is not made.
*/}}

{{- /*
	file_place hands over a parameter that is the file itself. The bytes travel in
	a part named by the parameter's own key, so the file writes into the sink,
	into the data, or into both, and this statement asks which nowhere: place is
	the question "put yourself under this key", and a file id and an upload answer
	it differently by being what they are.
*/}}
{{- define "file_place"}}{{/*gotype: github.com/andreychh/tgen/targets/pythonv2.Placed*/}}
{{- if .Optional}}
        if self.{{.Name}} is not None:
            self.{{.Name}}._place(sink, data, "{{.Key}}")
{{- else}}
        self.{{.Name}}._place(sink, data, "{{.Key}}")
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{- /*
	file_attach hands over a field that is the file itself. It owns no key of the
	request, so the bytes travel in a part the sink names and the reference to it is
	what takes the field's place. It names no key on the way in for the same reason:
	the part is one nothing else addresses, and the sink invents what to call it.
*/}}
{{- define "file_attach"}}{{/*gotype: github.com/andreychh/tgen/targets/pythonv2.Attached*/}}
{{- if .Optional}}
        if self.{{.Name}} is not None:
            data["{{.Key}}"] = self.{{.Name}}._attach(sink)
{{- else}}
        data["{{.Key}}"] = self.{{.Name}}._attach(sink)
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{- /*
	file_resolve hands over a field holding a file somewhere inside. It owns no
	part of the form, so it answers what JSON it becomes once its own files are
	handed over, and that answer takes its place in the data.
*/}}
{{- define "file_resolve"}}
{{- if .Optional}}
        if self.{{.Name}} is not None:
            data["{{.Key}}"] = self.{{.Name}}._resolve(sink)
{{- else}}
        data["{{.Key}}"] = self.{{.Name}}._resolve(sink)
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{- /*
	file_resolve_array does the same for a sequence, each element of which holds a
	file of its own and answers for itself.
*/}}
{{- define "file_resolve_array"}}
{{- if .Optional}}
        if self.{{.Name}} is not None:
            data["{{.Key}}"] = [el._resolve(sink) for el in self.{{.Name}}]
{{- else}}
        data["{{.Key}}"] = [el._resolve(sink) for el in self.{{.Name}}]
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
